24261474609375, -0.037384033203125, -0.024810791015625, 0.00312042236328125, -0.0006885528564453125, -0.00836944580078125, 0.0009822845458984375, 0.020965576171875, 0.0028324127197265625, 0.007106781005859375, -0.032745361328125, -0.0015344619750976562, 0.0296630859375, 0.0298004150390625, 0.005558013916015625, -0.0296478271484375, 0.022247314453125, -0.00478363037109375, 0.02056884765625, -0.0135955810546875, 0.0032138824462890625, -0.0004162788391113281, -0.0300445556640625, 0.0162200927734375, -0.004093170166015625, 0.0254058837890625, 0.0014591217041015625, 0.04052734375, 0.0281982421875, -0.00508880615234375, -0.0225677490234375, 0.005725860595703125, -0.0026378631591796875, -0.0122222900390625, 0.0119171142578125, 0.00862884521484375, -0.0239715576171875, 0.002796173095703125, -0.0199127197265625, -0.017913818359375, 0.0087127685546875, -0.0251312255859375, 0.01824951171875, -0.04486083984375, -0.0038890838623046875, -0.00797271728515625, -0.026641845703125, 0.0045166015625, -0.048431396484375, -0.006313323974609375, 0.0045928955078125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\nIntroduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents are learning systems that incrementally improve their performance by storing and retrieving information from a textual memory bank\u2014a structured repository of text-based experiences, solutions, or feedback accumulated over a sequence of tasks. These agents operate in an online setting, encountering a stream of tasks and leveraging their memory to adapt and enhance future performance. In practical deployments, such agents are expected to function reliably across a range of procedural variations, such as changes in task order or environment specification. However, their performance can be unexpectedly unstable under minor, non-adversarial changes in experimental conditions\u2014a phenomenon we term *fragility*.\n\n**Conceptual Distinctions**\n\nTo clarify terminology, Table 1 summarizes the key concepts used in this work:\n\n| Concept         | Definition                                                                 | Example Variation Tested | Operationalization in This Study                |\n|-----------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n| Fragility       | Sensitivity of agent performance to procedural/contextual variations that do not alter the underlying task distribution | Task order, environment specification | Standard deviation of task completion rate >5% across 5 runs, or mean difference between best and worst orderings >10% |\n| Robustness      | Resistance to distributional shifts or adversarial perturbations            | Out-of-distribution tasks, adversarial noise | Not directly tested here                        |\n| Generalization  | Performance on unseen data drawn from the same or related distribution      | Held-out tasks          | Not directly tested here                        |\n| Instability     | Stochastic fluctuations or randomness in training                           | Random seeds, initialization | Not the focus; refers to unsystematic variance  |\n\nFragility, as defined above, is distinct from robustness and generalization, which concern distributional changes and unseen data, respectively. Instability refers to random fluctuations, whereas fragility is systematic sensitivity to procedural details.\n\n**Operational Thresholds and Theoretical Justification:** An agent is considered \"fragile\" if the standard deviation of its task completion rate across five runs with different task orders exceeds 5% absolute, or if the mean difference between best and worst orderings exceeds 10% absolute. These thresholds are motivated by practical standards for reproducibility in machine learning benchmarks, where a 5% change in completion rate is often considered meaningful for deployment [1,2]. We also conducted a sensitivity analysis (Appendix Table 2) showing that results are qualitatively robust to threshold choices in the 3\u20137% (std dev) and 8\u201315% (mean diff) ranges. While no universal standard exists, these cutoffs align with prior work on reproducibility and reliability in agent evaluation [3].\n\n**Motivation and Related Work**\n\nStudying fragility is critical for two reasons: (1) In real-world applications, agents may encounter tasks in unpredictable orders or under slightly altered conditions, and high fragility undermines both user trust and reproducibility. (2) Prior work has focused on robustness and generalization, but the specific impact of procedural variations\u2014often overlooked\u2014can lead to misleading conclusions about agent reliability. Fragility thus represents a critical, underexplored failure mode that can persist even in otherwise robust or generalizable systems.\n\nRecent research on memory-augmented agents has primarily addressed improvements in sample efficiency [4], generalization to novel tasks [5], and robustness to adversarial perturbations [6]. However, few studies have systematically examined the sensitivity of these agents to benign procedural changes, such as task order or environment specification. Notably, [7] and [8] observed order effects in curriculum learning, but did not quantify their impact on memory-based self-improving agents. Our work extends this literature by providing a quantitative assessment of fragility in representative memory-augmented agents, highlighting a distinct and practically relevant failure mode.\n\n**Contributions**\n\nThis work provides a quantitative assessment of fragility and its contributing factors in representative memory-augmented agents. The primary aims are: (1) to systematically quantify the fragility of memory-based self-improving agents under varying task orders and environments, and (2) to identify and evaluate factors contributing to this fragility, with a focus on task and environment specification. We hypothesize that (a) agent performance exhibits high variance and sensitivity to task order, and (b) underspecification in tasks and environments is a primary contributor to this fragility.\n\nWhile we study only two agents and two environments, these represent distinct paradigms (supervised and RL; language and navigation tasks). Nevertheless, the limited scope restricts generalizability. Our findings may not extend to agents with different architectures (e.g., transformer-based RL agents, memory networks), other task domains (e.g., vision, planning), or more complex environments. Future work should expand the evaluation to a broader set of agents and tasks, including hierarchical memory systems and real-world deployment scenarios.\n\nMethods\n\n**Experimental Protocol Overview**\n\nTo improve clarity, the experimental design is summarized in Figure 1 (see Appendix), and key steps are outlined below:\n\n1. **Agent Selection:** We evaluate two representative memory-based self-improving agents:\n   - Agent A: Retrieval-augmented language model (fine-tuned T5-Base) with a key-value memory bank storing (task description, solution, feedback) tuples.\n   - Agent B: Memory-augmented reinforcement learning agent (PPO backbone with LSTM-based memory module for textual feedback and solutions).\n   These architectures are widely used baselines in language modeling and reinforcement learning, respectively, and have been adopted in prior memory-augmented agent research [4,5]. While only two agents are studied, they represent distinct paradigms (supervised and RL) and memory mechanisms. We acknowledge this limits generalizability; future work should expand to additional architectures.\n\n2. **Environment Selection:** We use ALFWorld (household manipulation; 20 tasks) and BabyAI (navigation and instruction following; 15 tasks), chosen for their diversity in task structure, established use in benchmarking [6,7], and support for multi-step reasoning and language-based interaction. These environments cover distinct modalities but do not exhaust the space of possible tasks.\n\n3. **Baseline Agents:** For Agent A, the baseline is a T5-Base model without memory; for Agent B, a PPO agent with the memory module ablated. All agents are trained and evaluated under identical conditions.\n\n4. **Task Sampling:** Tasks are randomly sampled from the standard ALFWorld and BabyAI benchmarks, stratified by environment-provided difficulty ratings (easy, medium, hard) to ensure coverage (\u226530% per level).\n\n5. **Task Order Randomization:** For each run, the task sequence is shuffled using five fixed random seeds (42, 101, 202, 303, 404) to ensure reproducibility and sample plausible orderings.\n\n6. **Training and Evaluation:**\n   - Each agent is trained for 100,000 episodes per environment.\n   - Early stopping: Training halts if no improvement in average reward is observed on a held-out validation set (10% of tasks) for 10,000 episodes.\n   - Hyperparameter tuning: Grid search over learning rate [1e-5, 3e-5, 1e-4], batch size [8, 16, 32], memory size [50, 100, 200], and retrieval threshold [0.7, 0.75, 0.8]. Best configuration selected by average reward.\n   - For each task, agents are allowed up to 5 episodes to attempt completion.\n   - Performance metrics: Task completion rate (percentage of tasks solved) and average reward per episode.\n\n7. **Memory Analysis:** For each agent and environment, 60 memory entries (stratified by task type) are randomly sampled. Two independent, blinded raters annotate each entry using a rubric with three criteria:\n   - **Specificity:** How precisely the entry describes the task or solution (high: detailed and unambiguous; medium: partially detailed; low: vague).\n   - **Relevance:** How directly the entry applies to the current task (high: directly applicable; medium: partially applicable; low: unrelated).\n   - **Completeness:** Whether the entry contains all necessary information for successful execution (high: fully sufficient; medium: missing minor details; low: missing key information).\n   Inter-rater reliability (Cohen's kappa): specificity 0.89, relevance 0.85, completeness 0.88. Disagreements resolved via structured discussion.\n\n   To link memory entry quality to agent performance, we computed Pearson correlations between the proportion of high-quality (high specificity, relevance, completeness) entries and task completion rates across runs. We also performed linear regression analyses to assess the predictive value of memory quality on performance, controlling for agent and environment.\n\n8. **Statistical Analysis Plan:** For each agent/environment, we report mean, standard deviation, and 95% confidence intervals for task completion rate and average reward across five runs. Differences across task orderings are assessed using one-way ANOVA (for >2 groups, assuming normality and homogeneity of variance) or paired t-tests (for two groups, assuming normality), with Holm-Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons. Effect sizes (Cohen's d, \u03b7\u00b2) and sample sizes are reported for all comparisons. Where assumptions are violated, non-parametric alternatives (Kruskal-Wallis, Wilcoxon signed-rank) are used and noted.\n\n**Implementation Details:** Hyperparameters for all agents are detailed in Appendix Table 1. Experiments were conducted on NVIDIA V100 GPUs with PyTorch 1.10.\n\nResults\n\n**Fragility Quantification**\n\nTable 2 summarizes task completion rates and variance across five task orderings for each agent and environment.\n\n| Agent   | Environment | Mean Completion Rate (%) | Std Dev (%) | Best-Worst Diff (%) | Fragile? |\n|---------|-------------|-------------------------|-------------|---------------------|----------|\n| Agent A | ALFWorld    | 52.1                    |"}}